  <article class="article">
    <h1>Regulatory Scope &amp; Local Compliance</h1>
    <p><em>Last updated: 2026-11-24 · Applies to incensehub.org and data.incensehub.org</em></p>

    <blockquote>
      IncenseHub publishes information about <strong>incense products manufactured in China</strong>
      and the Chinese botanical-incense tradition. That information is educational and general. It is
      not a product-specific compliance certificate, and it does not establish that a product may be
      lawfully imported, sold or used in every country. Product form, ingredients, claims, labels,
      import route and the destination's current law all affect the answer.
    </blockquote>

    <h2>1. What this site covers</h2>
    <p>
      This site is an English-language reference for Chinese botanical incense: ingredients, sticks,
      cones, coils, powders, resins, history, manufacturing, sourcing and wholesale practice. Its
      examples are written around products manufactured in China and the conventions of the Chinese
      incense trade.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site is edited and operated from China for an international readership. It is not a
      substitute for the law, labelling rules, import requirements or product-safety review of the
      country where a product is made, sold, imported or used.
    </p>

    <h2>2. What this site does not claim</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It does not certify that any product complies with the law of any country.</li>
      <li>It does not cover products made outside China, or Chinese products reformulated, repackaged or relabelled for another market.</li>
      <li>It does not determine customs classification, duty, import licensing or market access.</li>
      <li>It does not approve health, therapeutic, cosmetic, antimicrobial or pest-control claims.</li>
      <li>It does not replace a supplier's SDS, COA, label, test report or local legal advice.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Why the same incense can be regulated differently</h2>
    <p>
      "Incense" and "香薰" cover several product forms. A combustible stick, an essential-oil blend, a
      reed diffuser, a room spray, a candle and a botanical powder can fall under different rules even
      when they share a scent. The regulatory question usually turns on:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Product form and intended use</strong> — burning, ambient fragrance, skin contact or ritual use.</li>
      <li><strong>Ingredients</strong> — botanical species, resins, essential oils, synthetic fragrance, solvents and allergens.</li>
      <li><strong>Claims</strong> — decorative fragrance, cosmetic, therapeutic, antimicrobial or pest-control.</li>
      <li><strong>Channel</strong> — consumer retail, professional trade, wholesale, OEM or import.</li>
      <li><strong>Destination</strong> — national and state/provincial product-safety, chemical, labelling, fire and environmental rules.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Product-form starting points</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Product form</th>
          <th>Typical regulatory focus</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Incense sticks, cones, coils and combustible powder</td>
          <td>Consumer-product safety, fire safety, smoke and ventilation warnings, packaging, labelling and local air-quality or sales restrictions.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Essential oils and fragrance blends</td>
          <td>Chemical classification and labelling, fragrance allergens, child-resistant packaging, cosmetics rules if applied to skin, and medicine rules if therapeutic claims are made.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Reed diffusers, room sprays and gels</td>
          <td>Flammability, chemical labelling, child and pet safety, VOC/air-freshener rules and packaging.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Candles</td>
          <td>Candle safety standards, fire warnings, fragrance labelling and consumer-product rules.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Raw botanical materials and resins</td>
          <td>Species identity, CITES and plant-health rules, customs, pesticide and contaminant controls, and destination import requirements.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>5. Country and region starting points</h2>
    <p>
      The table below lists frameworks to check, not conclusions that a product complies. Local law,
      state/provincial rules and the product's actual claims control.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Market</th>
          <th>Examples of frameworks to verify</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>China</td>
          <td>Product-quality and consumer-safety requirements, domestic sales rules, CITES/endangered-species controls, export and customs requirements.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>United States</td>
          <td>CPSC consumer-product safety, Federal Hazardous Substances Act, state warnings such as California Proposition 65, FDA/FTC rules where cosmetic or drug claims are made, CITES and customs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Canada</td>
          <td>Canada Consumer Product Safety Act, Consumer Chemicals and Containers Regulations, bilingual labelling, Health Canada requirements for cosmetic or health claims, CITES and import controls.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>United Kingdom</td>
          <td>General product-safety rules, UK REACH and CLP where applicable, cosmetic and medicine-claim rules, fire-safety warnings and import requirements.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>European Union</td>
          <td>General Product Safety Regulation (EU) 2023/988, REACH, CLP, the Cosmetics Regulation where applicable, fragrance-allergen labelling, CITES and national market rules.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Australia</td>
          <td>Australian Consumer Law and product-safety requirements, AICIS industrial-chemical rules, TGA rules if therapeutic claims are made, state/territory fire and environmental rules.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Japan</td>
          <td>Consumer Product Safety Act, household-goods labelling, fire and flammable-goods rules, and pharmaceutical/medical-device rules if therapeutic claims are made.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>GCC / Middle East</td>
          <td>SASO/SABER or national conformity schemes, GSO standards, Arabic labelling, restricted-ingredient and import requirements.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>6. Ingredients that need extra verification</h2>
    <p>
      Some incense materials carry rules beyond ordinary consumer-product labelling:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Agarwood (Aquilaria species)</strong> — the genus is listed in CITES Appendix II. Trade may require permits and origin documentation.</li>
      <li><strong>Red sandalwood (Pterocarpus santalinus)</strong> — CITES Appendix II; verify species and permits.</li>
      <li><strong>Essential oils</strong> — fragrance allergens, oxidation, flammability and concentration limits can trigger chemical or cosmetic labelling.</li>
      <li><strong>Resins and gums</strong> — identity, purity, pesticide and contaminant controls, and import restrictions vary by market.</li>
      <li><strong>Any material with a therapeutic claim</strong> — may move the product into a medicine or therapeutic-goods category.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      "Natural" does not mean unrestricted. A botanical can be a recognised fragrance material, a
      restricted substance, a CITES-listed species, or all three in different contexts.
    </p>

    <h2>7. For importers, retailers and brands</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Classify the actual product form and intended use before choosing a label.</li>
      <li>Verify the destination's product-safety, chemical, cosmetic, fire and environmental rules.</li>
      <li>Obtain SDS, COA, ingredient disclosure and species/origin documents where relevant.</li>
      <li>Check claims: fragrance, cosmetic, therapeutic, antimicrobial and pest-control claims are not interchangeable.</li>
      <li>Confirm import licences, customs classification, labelling language and responsible economic operator.</li>
      <li>Keep batch records and a route for recalls or corrections.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>8. For buyers sourcing from China</h2>
    <p>
      A Chinese supplier's product may be lawful to manufacture and export while still requiring
      changes to label, claims, packaging or documentation for the destination. Ask the supplier for
      the specification, batch COA, SDS, ingredient list, species/origin declarations and packaging
      information. Do not treat this website, a supplier catalogue or a generic certificate as a
      market-access approval.
    </p>

    <h2>9. If you need a definitive answer</h2>
    <p>
      Use the destination regulator's current text, a qualified local lawyer or a compliance
      consultant, and the actual product specification. Where this site summarises a rule, the
      official text controls. If you believe a page is inaccurate or out of date, use our
      <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> and include the URL, sentence and official source.
    </p>

    <p class="muted">
      This page is a scope and responsibility statement. It is not legal advice and does not create
